<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eduard Bernstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eduard Bernstein was a German social democratic politician and socialist theorist, known for the reformist challenge to Marxism called evolutionary socialism or revisionism. Born into a lower-middle-class Jewish family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, he lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a> and became a close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>. In Evolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a> (1899) he disputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>'s predictions of the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and argued for gradual reform through democratic institutions. The SPD condemned his views but largely followed them in practice. He co-founded the anti-war Independent Social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">USPD</a>) during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, and later sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein.jpg" alt="Eduard Bernstein" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eduard_Bernstein_(portrait).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 20th century <b>Eduard Bernstein</b>, influenced by the gradualism of the Fabian movement in Britain, rejected orthodox Marxist ideas of the inevitable progress of history and the need for revolution: socialism, he argued, should rest on ethical arguments and come through gradual legislative reform.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Rosa Luxemburg and <b>Eduard Bernstein</b> called Lenin’s idea of revolution elitist; Hal Draper set a socialism from below against socialism from above; Luxemburg held that reforms strengthen capitalism, while André Gorz proposed “non-reformist reforms”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
